meta {
  name: Move Item
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/items/{{itemId}}/move
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "workphaseId": "{{workphaseId}}",
    "anchorId": "{{anchorId}}",
    "side": "after"
  }
}

docs {
  # Move Item

  Primary shape: `anchorId` + `side` ("before" | "after") — the server
  resolves the actual position against the anchor's true current neighbor.
  Omit `anchorId`/`side` to append to the end of the target scope.

  `workphaseId` alone (no anchor) is a distinct case: it keeps the item's
  existing `position` unchanged rather than appending — position is
  workflow-scoped, so changing only the workphase doesn't invalidate the
  existing value. This is also the shape the MCP `move_item` tool sends.

  A raw `position` (finite number) is still accepted instead of
  anchorId+side, but anchor+side is the documented, preferred way to
  reposition an item — see `docs/wiki/item-positioning/README.md` §2.

  ## Hierarchy (Epic 3.5)

  `parentId` (string, or `null` for "make top-level") and `isAttached`
  (boolean) can ride along with any of the above and commit in the same
  transaction — e.g. the board's "drop Card onto Card" gesture sends
  `{ parentId, isAttached: true, anchorId, side }`. Rules:

  - Omitting `isAttached` keeps the item's current state; nothing attaches
    by default. `isAttached: true` with no resulting parent is a 400.
  - An attached item inherits its parent's workphase; sending a different
    `workphaseId` alongside is a 400.
  - Cross-project parents are allowed — `projectId` is derived from the
    parent and cascades to the whole subtree. Never send `projectId`.
  - Rejected for a parent that doesn't exist, is the item itself or one of
    its descendants, or would put the resulting tree past the depth cap.
  - `anchorId` is validated against the scope the item will be in *after*
    the reparent (a new sibling under the target parent is a legal anchor).

  The response is the item row plus, only when a reindex fired, a
  `reindexed: [{id, position}]` collection covering every renumbered item.
  See `docs/wiki/item-attachment/README.md` §1, §4–§11a.
}
